        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Walking up to a row of gym treadmills for the first time can be oddly intimidating. The
          consoles are bigger than a home machine’s, there are more buttons than you need, and
          someone is always running effortlessly on the next belt. The good news is that the
          essentials are the same on nearly every commercial treadmill: a safety clip, a start or
          quick-start button, arrows for speed and incline, and a large stop button. Learn those and
          you can use whatever your club has. Where the console supports it, you can also tap an
          Apple Watch to the machine and record the session properly.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Use a Planet Fitness Treadmill: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Stand on the side rails, clip the safety cord to your clothing, press Quick Start
            or Start, and raise the speed slowly with the arrows until you are walking comfortably.
            Use the incline arrows to add a slope, and press Stop — or pull the clip — to end.</strong>
            Wipe the machine down when you finish. That sequence works on almost every commercial
            treadmill, whichever make your club has.
          </p>
          <p>
            Planet Fitness clubs do not all use the same treadmills. Its annual report says clubs use
            Planet Fitness-branded equipment from “leading suppliers”, that most clubs are run by
            franchisees, and that equipment is replaced every five to nine years, so consoles differ
            between clubs and over time. The button names below are the common ones; if your console
            looks different, the start and stop buttons will still be the largest and easiest to
            find, and staff can show you the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            To connect an Apple Watch, look for “Connects to Apple Watch” on the console, turn on
            Detect Gym Equipment in the Workout settings on the watch, and hold the watch close to the
            console’s contactless reader until it taps and beeps. If your treadmill does not have it,
            start an Indoor Walk or Indoor Run on the watch instead. If you have never used a
            treadmill at all, our
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">step-by-step guide to using a treadmill</a>
            covers a first session in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-treadmills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What Treadmills Does Planet Fitness Use?</h2>
<p>
            There is no single answer, and anyone who gives you one without a date and a club attached
            is guessing. This is what we could confirm.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Planet Fitness says</h3>
          <p>
            Planet Fitness’s annual report for 2025 says it provides “Planet Fitness-branded fitness
            equipment from leading suppliers”, stipulates the specific pieces and quantities of
            cardio and strength equipment in its clubs, and approves club layouts. Franchisees must
            buy their fitness equipment through Planet Fitness, or from required vendors in certain
            international markets, and generally replace it every five to nine years depending on how
            busy the club is. At the end of 2025 the company reported 2,896 clubs, of which 2,604 were
            franchisee-owned. It does not name its current treadmill suppliers.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the equipment makers have said</h3>
          <p>
            The clearest public record is from 2018. Life Fitness, in a filing with the US Securities
            and Exchange Commission, said Planet Fitness had been its largest customer under an
            exclusive contract, and that the new contract agreed in 2018 was not exclusive and allowed
            Planet Fitness to buy from Life Fitness or two of its competitors. Brunswick, which owned
            Life Fitness at the time, said the same in its annual report. Neither filing names the two
            competitors, and we could not confirm from a company source which makes went into which
            clubs after that.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What that means for you</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Your club may have more than one make,</strong> especially if it has been
            re-equipped in stages or has machines of different ages.</li>
            <li><strong>Two clubs can feel different.</strong> Consoles, belt feel and the exact
            button layout vary by make and model.</li>
            <li><strong>The maker’s name is on the machine.</strong> It is usually on the console or
            the motor cover. Knowing it helps if you want to look up that console’s features.</li>
            <li><strong>Franchise clubs set some things locally.</strong> Opening hours, house rules
            and how many treadmills a club has can differ between clubs.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Before You Step On</h2>
<p>
            A minute of preparation makes the first session far less awkward.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Pick a machine with space behind it.</strong> You want clear floor behind the
            belt, not a wall or a weight bench.</li>
            <li><strong>Check it is working.</strong> If the console is dark when others are lit, or
            there is an out-of-order sign, choose another and tell the front desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Wear trainers.</strong> Soft, flat-soled shoes with laces tied. No sandals,
            and not in socks.</li>
            <li><strong>Bring water and a towel.</strong> Most consoles have a bottle holder and a
            ledge for a phone.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the stop button and the safety clip before the belt moves.</strong>
            The stop is usually a large red button in the middle of the console. The clip hangs on a
            cord nearby.</li>
            <li><strong>Stand on the side rails.</strong> Put one foot on each rail either side of
            the belt, not on the belt itself, before you press anything.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are not sure how hard to go, plan to walk, not run, for your first few sessions. A
            gym treadmill will happily go much faster than you need.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ask for a walk-through</h3>
          <p>
            Planet Fitness’s annual report says its clubs have staff trainers who offer free fitness
            instruction to members in small groups. What that looks like varies by club, so ask at
            the front desk. Even a two-minute demonstration of the console in front of you — where
            Start, Stop and the clip are, and how the incline works — is worth more than any general
            guide, because it is the exact machine you will use. There is no shame in asking; staff
            show people the treadmills every day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="starting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Starting the Belt: Quick Start, Start and Programmes</h2>
<p>
            Commercial consoles offer two ways to begin: a quick start that just gets the belt moving,
            or a guided setup where you choose a programme first.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Quick Start</h3>
          <p>
            Look for a button marked Quick Start, Start or Go, usually green. Pressing it starts the
            belt at a slow walking speed after a short countdown, and the timer begins. This is the
            easiest way in: you then change speed and incline as you go. On touchscreen consoles the
            same option is often a large tile on the home screen.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Programmes and goals</h3>
          <p>
            Most consoles also offer built-in programmes — hills, intervals, fat-burn or heart-rate
            workouts — and goal settings such as time, distance or calories. They typically ask for
            your weight, age and a duration first. Programmes change the speed or incline for you,
            which is fine once you are comfortable, but can surprise you on your first visit. Start
            with Quick Start, and try a programme once you know where the controls are.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If the belt will not start</h3>
          <p>
            Check that the safety clip is in place on the console; most treadmills will not start
            without it. Check the console is not paused or waiting for you to finish a setup screen.
            If it still will not start, use another machine and tell staff. Do not try to restart it
            from the power switch.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Touchscreen consoles</h3>
          <p>
            Newer treadmills often have a large touchscreen with entertainment, virtual routes and
            app logins as well as the workout controls. It can look like a tablet more than a
            treadmill, but the essentials are still there: a start tile or button, speed and incline
            controls on screen or on the handlebars, and a physical Stop button below the screen. You
            can ignore everything else. If you log in to an app on a shared console, log out when you
            finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Speed and Incline Controls</h2>
<p>
            Speed and incline usually sit on opposite sides of the console, so you can change one
            with each hand. Most consoles show speed in miles per hour in the US and incline as a
            percentage grade.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Changing speed</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Arrows or plus and minus buttons</strong> change speed in small steps,
            usually a tenth of a mile per hour each press. Hold to change faster.</li>
            <li><strong>Number keys or quick-speed buttons</strong> on many consoles jump straight
            to a set speed. They are convenient, but a big jump can catch you out; until you are
            confident, use the arrows.</li>
            <li><strong>Some machines have controls on the handrails</strong> as well as the
            console, which is handy once you are moving.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            As a rough guide, walking speeds typically fall somewhere between about 2.5 and 3.5 miles
            per hour, with a brisk walk a little above that, but your pace is your own. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-speed-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what speed to walk on a treadmill</a>
            helps you find yours.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Changing incline</h3>
          <p>
            The incline buttons raise the front of the deck so the walk becomes a climb. Change it a
            step at a time and expect it to take a few seconds to move. A useful rule: when you raise
            the incline, lower the speed a little, because the same pace becomes much harder on a
            slope. Commercial treadmills usually offer a wide incline range, and some also decline;
            the display shows where you are.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Reading the display</h3>
          <p>
            Most consoles show time, distance, speed, incline, an estimated calorie count and, if you
            hold the hand sensors or wear a compatible strap, heart rate. Calorie figures are
            estimates. Use them to compare your own sessions, not as a measurement.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Heart rate on the console</h3>
          <p>
            Many commercial treadmills have metal grip sensors on the handrails that read your heart
            rate while you hold them. They are handy for an occasional check, but holding them
            changes how you walk, and they are less reliable than a chest strap or a watch. Some
            consoles can also receive heart rate wirelessly from a compatible strap or, on consoles
            that support it, from a paired Apple Watch. If heart rate matters to your training, a
            strap or watch is the better source; glance at the grip reading, then let go.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stopping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Stopping, Pausing and the Safety Clip</h2>
<p>
            Knowing how to stop is more important than knowing how to start.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Normal finish:</strong> lower the speed to a slow walk for a few minutes,
            bring the incline back to zero, then press Stop. Many consoles offer a Cool Down button
            that does this for you.</li>
            <li><strong>Pause:</strong> some consoles have a Pause button that stops the belt but
            keeps your session data for a short time. Step onto the side rails while paused.</li>
            <li><strong>Emergency:</strong> press the large red Stop button, or pull the safety clip.
            Either brings the belt to a halt.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why the clip matters</h3>
          <p>
            The safety clip is attached to a cord that pulls a key or magnet out of the console if you
            drift back or fall. The belt then stops. It only works if it is clipped to your clothing,
            at your waist, before you start. It may feel unnecessary at walking pace; it is exactly
            when you are not expecting to stumble that it earns its place.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stepping off</h3>
          <p>
            Wait until the belt has completely stopped before you step off, and step off to the side
            rails rather than the back. Never jump off a moving belt, and never step back onto one
            that is running fast. If you feel dizzy, hold the handrails, slow the belt and stop.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The land-legs feeling</h3>
          <p>
            After a treadmill session, the floor can feel briefly strange underfoot, as if it is
            moving. It is a common, short-lived sensation as your brain readjusts to solid ground,
            and a few minutes of slow walking at the end of the session makes it milder. Step off
            carefully and stand for a moment before you walk away. Dizziness that does not pass
            quickly, or that comes with other symptoms, is a reason to sit down, tell staff and get
            it checked.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="etiquette" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Busy Times, Etiquette and Wiping Down</h2>
<p>
            A gym treadmill is shared equipment, and a few habits make it easier for everyone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Busy times</h3>
          <p>
            Treadmills are among the most popular machines in any gym, and evenings and weekends tend
            to be busiest. Many gyms ask members to keep cardio sessions reasonable when people are
            waiting, and some post a time limit for peak hours. We could not confirm Planet Fitness’s
            current rule, and franchise clubs may set their own house rules, so follow the signs in
            your club or ask at the front desk. If you want a long session, go at a quieter time.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wiping down</h3>
          <p>
            Wipe the handrails, the console and any part you touched or dripped on when you finish,
            using the cleaning spray and towels your club provides. Spray the towel, not the console
            screen, and do not spray into the motor cover. It takes a minute and is the most noticed
            courtesy in a gym.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Other courtesies</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Use headphones for music or video.</li>
            <li>Keep bags off the floor behind the treadmill, where someone could step back onto
            them.</li>
            <li>Leave a gap between you and a busy runner if there is a free machine further
            along.</li>
            <li>If someone is clearly waiting, let them know roughly how long you have left; it
            avoids an awkward stand-off.</li>
            <li>Take your bottle, towel and any wipes with you when you go, and return the incline
            to zero so the next person starts on the flat.</li>
            <li>Report a machine that is slipping, jerking or making unusual noises, rather than
            leaving it for the next person.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="apple-watch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. How to Connect an Apple Watch to a Planet Fitness Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Some commercial treadmills can pair directly with an Apple Watch, using Apple’s system for
            gym equipment, often called GymKit. When it works, the watch records the treadmill’s own
            distance, speed and incline, and the console can show your heart rate. Whether a Planet
            Fitness treadmill supports it depends on the console fitted to that machine, not on the
            gym, so check each treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to tell if a treadmill supports it</h3>
          <p>
            Apple says compatible equipment shows “Connects to Apple Watch” or “Connect to Apple
            Watch”. Look for the wording on the console, near the contactless reader. As one example,
            Life Fitness says all three of its current consoles — SL, SE4 and Symbio — support Apple
            Watch connectivity. We could not confirm which consoles are fitted in any particular Planet
            Fitness club.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pairing steps, from Apple’s instructions</h3>
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>On the watch, open Settings, tap Workout, and turn on Detect Gym Equipment.</li>
            <li>With the treadmill ready but not yet started, hold the watch within a few centimetres
            of the contactless reader, display facing the reader.</li>
            <li>Wait for a gentle tap and a beep, which confirm the watch is paired.</li>
            <li>Press Start on the treadmill to begin, and control the session from the console as
            normal.</li>
            <li>Press Stop on the treadmill to end the workout; the watch ends with it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If Detect Gym Equipment is switched off, Apple says you can open the Workout app first
            and then hold the watch to the reader. Menu names move between watchOS versions; if
            something is not where we say, check Apple’s current support page.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">No “Connects to Apple Watch”? Start a workout by hand</h3>
          <p>
            Every treadmill works with the manual method. Open the Workout app and choose Indoor Walk
            or Indoor Run before you start the belt; Apple’s guidance is to pick the workout type
            that matches what you are doing, and Indoor Run for running on a treadmill. Without the
            treadmill’s data, the watch estimates distance from your arm movement, and its estimate
            and the console’s figure will often differ. Apple says calibrating the watch outdoors — an
            Outdoor Walk or Run on flat, open ground for about 20 minutes — helps it learn your stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our full guide to
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
            explains why the two distances disagree and how to get cleaner data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="workouts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Planet Fitness Treadmill Workouts to Start With</h2>
<p>
            You do not need a programme to get a good session. These four use only Quick Start and the
            speed and incline arrows, so they work on any treadmill in any club. Times and grades are
            starting points; adjust them to how you feel.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">1. The first-visit walk (20 minutes)</h3>
          <p>
            Five minutes at an easy pace on the flat, ten minutes at a pace where you can still talk
            in full sentences, five minutes easing back down. Do this two or three times before you
            add anything. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-beginners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">beginner treadmill workouts</a>
            build on it week by week.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">2. The steady incline walk (30 minutes)</h3>
          <p>
            Warm up for five minutes on the flat. Raise the incline a step at a time to a moderate
            slope you can hold without the handrails, lowering the speed as you go. Hold it for 20
            minutes, then spend five minutes back on the flat. It is hard work without the impact of
            running.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">3. The 12-3-30 workout</h3>
          <p>
            The 12-3-30 is the best-known treadmill walk online: 12% incline, 3 miles per hour, for
            30 minutes. It is a genuinely hard session for most people, and the commonest mistake is
            doing it on day one while gripping the rails. Build up to it over several weeks with a
            lower incline or shorter time. Our
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 treadmill workout guide</a>
            has a four-week build-up and modifications that still count.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">4. Simple intervals (25 minutes)</h3>
          <p>
            After a five-minute warm-up, alternate one minute brisk — a faster walk, or a jog if you
            already run — with two minutes easy, five or six times, then cool down. Use the arrows,
            not the quick-speed keys, so changes stay gradual. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill interval workouts</a>
            take this further.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How hard should it feel?</h3>
          <p>
            The simplest guide is the talk test. On an easy walk you can chat in full sentences. On a
            moderate session you can talk, but in shorter sentences. On a hard interval or a steep
            climb you can only manage a few words at a time. Most of your treadmill time, especially
            in the first month, should sit in the easy-to-moderate range. If you are gasping, dizzy
            or have to hold the rails to keep going, slow down or lower the incline.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How often</h3>
          <p>
            Two or three sessions a week is a sensible start for most new gym members, with a rest
            or easy day between harder ones. Change one thing at a time — a few more minutes, a
            little more incline or a slightly faster pace — rather than all three in the same week.
            Steep incline work in particular loads the calves and Achilles tendons, so add it
            gradually and keep steep sessions off consecutive days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gym-vs-home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Gym Treadmill or Home Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Club treadmills are commercial machines, built to run for many hours a day with many
            users, and they generally feel steadier, longer and quieter than a typical home treadmill.
            They also cost nothing extra once you have paid for a membership, and the gym handles the
            maintenance. The trade-off is travel time, busy periods and sharing.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you find you are using the treadmill most visits, it is reasonable to wonder whether a
            treadmill at home would be cheaper or more convenient. The answer depends on how often you
            would really use it, the space you have and what else you go to the gym for. Our
            <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home treadmill vs gym membership</a>
            comparison works through the arithmetic. Using a gym treadmill for a few months first is
            also one of the best ways to learn what you would want from one at home: the belt length
            you are comfortable on, the incline you use and the features you never touch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be aware that a home treadmill at a modest price will not feel like the club machine. It
            will usually have a shorter, narrower belt, a lighter frame and a smaller incline range,
            and it may feel bouncier at running pace. That is not a fault; it is the difference
            between a machine built for one household and one built for a busy gym floor. Try the
            home model in person if you can before you buy it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-things-go-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. If Something Goes Wrong on a Gym Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Commercial treadmills are robust, but busy machines still develop faults. You are not
            expected to fix them; you are expected to stop and say something.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>The belt slips or jerks under your foot.</strong> Slow down, stop and move to
            another machine. Tell staff, because a slipping belt can throw you off balance.</li>
            <li><strong>The speed changes on its own.</strong> Pull the safety clip, step to the side
            rails and report it.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt does not stop when you pull the clip or press Stop.</strong> Step to
            the side rails, hold the handrails and get staff immediately. Do not use that machine
            again until it has been checked.</li>
            <li><strong>A burning smell, smoke or a loud grinding noise.</strong> Stop, step off and
            tell staff.</li>
            <li><strong>The display freezes.</strong> Stop the belt with the Stop button or the clip,
            and use another treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Your watch will not pair.</strong> Check Detect Gym Equipment is on, that the
            treadmill has not already started, and that the console carries “Connects to Apple
            Watch”. If not, start an Indoor Walk or Run by hand.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of these is your fault, and staff would much rather hear about a machine than find
            out about it from an injured member.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you start a Planet Fitness treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stand on the side rails, clip the safety cord to your clothing, then press the Quick Start, Start or Go button, usually green. The belt starts slowly after a short countdown. Step on, then use the speed arrows to build up gradually. If it will not start, check the safety clip is seated on the console, and if that is not it, use another machine and tell staff.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you connect an Apple Watch to a Planet Fitness treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the console says Connects to Apple Watch. On the watch, turn on Detect Gym Equipment under Settings, then Workout. Before starting the belt, hold the watch close to the contactless reader, display facing it, until it taps and beeps. Press Start on the treadmill. If the console lacks the feature, start an Indoor Walk or Indoor Run on the watch instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What brand of treadmill does Planet Fitness use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies by club. Planet Fitness says it uses Planet Fitness-branded equipment from leading suppliers and does not name them. Life Fitness said in a 2019 filing that its contract with Planet Fitness had become non-exclusive in 2018, allowing purchases from Life Fitness or two competitors. Clubs re-equip every five to nine years, so check the maker's name on the console.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a time limit on Planet Fitness treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We could not confirm Planet Fitness's current policy, because its website blocked our checks, and franchise clubs can set their own house rules. Many gyms ask members to keep cardio sessions reasonable when others are waiting, and some post limits for peak hours. Check the signs in your club or ask at the front desk, and go at quieter times for long sessions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you do the 12-3-30 workout on a Planet Fitness treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most commercial treadmills, yes, because their incline ranges usually go well beyond 12%; check the incline display on your machine. The workout is 12% incline at 3 miles per hour for 30 minutes, which is hard for most people. Build up over several weeks with a lower incline or a shorter time, and do it without holding the handrails.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good Planet Fitness treadmill workout for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with 20 minutes: five minutes easy on the flat, ten minutes at a pace where you can still talk in full sentences, then five minutes easing down. Repeat it two or three times a week for a couple of weeks before adding incline or intervals. Use Quick Start and the arrows rather than programmes until you know the controls.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes on a Gym Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most first-session problems on a gym treadmill come down to one of these four, and all of them are easy to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting with your feet on the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Standing on the belt when you press Start means the first movement can pull a foot back and unbalance you. Stand on the side rails, start the belt at its slowest speed, then step on with one foot, then the other, holding the handrail lightly. Build up the speed only once you are walking comfortably.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the safety clip hanging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The clip only stops the belt if it is attached to you. Left dangling on the console, it does nothing when you stumble. Clip it to your waistband or the hem of your top before the belt moves, every session, and leave enough slack that normal arm swing does not pull it out.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the quick-speed keys too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Number keys and quick-speed buttons can jump the belt several miles per hour at once. On a machine you do not know, that is a fast way to lose your footing. Use the arrows for gradual changes until you are confident, and always check the speed display before you press a preset.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the rails on an incline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Gripping the handrails or console on a steep incline takes much of the work off your legs and changes your posture, so the session is easier than the display says. If you cannot let go, the incline or speed is too high for now. Lower one of them until you can walk hands-free.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

